<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert I, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1035–<a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Robert the Frisian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert the Frisian was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1071 to 1093. A younger son of Baldwin V, he gained his name by marrying Gertrude of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, widow of the count of Holland, and governing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>. He seized Flanders from his nephew Arnulf III and sister-in-law Richilde at the Battle of Cassel in 1071, later made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and was father of Robert II, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Robert I, Count of Flanders" width="285" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olivier De Wree,Lambertus Vossius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_I_of_Flanders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
